From 8bd4f918c4167a1845facf7df5725a6b53d16a9f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob Paprocki <hi@rpaprocki.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 22:46:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 19/26] ui: say what the API server, the connection type and
 the password are for

A user's clients never appeared in the console: the form's API server
was http://<name> behind Cloudflare, which redirects http to https, and
a client cannot follow that redirect when it checks in (a redirected
POST becomes a GET). The same user put the permanent password into the
build for the computer they connect from, so the computer they connect
to kept asking for its one-time password or an Accept click. And Mac
builds quietly used most of their GitHub Actions minutes.

- API server: the hint says the client checks in there (that is how it
  appears under Devices with its status); a warning shows while it is
  http:// with a host name that is not an IP address or a LAN name.
- Connection Type: what Incoming Only, Outgoing Only and Bidirectional
  are for.
- Permanent password: it belongs in the build for the computers you
  connect to; a live note says when it can never be used (Outgoing
  Only, approve mode "via click") or when its absence means one-time
  passwords and Accept clicks.
- Platform: what a build costs in Actions minutes; a Mac build runs two
  Mac machines and uses roughly 600-700 of the Free plan's 2,000.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AG6wkRApDVwXPoE7rdKoh6
